---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import FlexboxGenerator from '../../components/FlexboxGenerator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="CSS Flexbox Generator" description="Visually configure flex-direction, justify-content, align-items, and gap with a live preview.">
  <FlexboxGenerator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why flexbox properties are easy to mix up</h2>
  <p>justify-content and align-items both control alignment, but along different axes — and which axis is "main" versus "cross" flips depending on flex-direction, which is the single most common source of flexbox confusion.</p>

  <h2>How to use this generator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Set flex-direction (row or column) first — this determines your main axis.</li>
    <li>Adjust justify-content (main axis) and align-items (cross axis) with live preview.</li>
    <li>Copy the generated CSS.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>A horizontally and vertically centered set of items uses <code>display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center;</code> — a combination so common it's become a running joke about flexbox's necessity for basic centering.</p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Confusing justify-content and align-items</strong> after changing flex-direction — swapping to column flips which property controls which visual axis.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting flex-wrap</strong> when items need to wrap onto multiple lines — without it, flex items will shrink or overflow rather than wrapping.</li>
    <li><strong>Applying flex properties to the wrong element</strong> — container properties (justify-content, align-items) go on the parent; item properties (flex-grow, align-self) go on the children.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Centering content both horizontally and vertically</li>
    <li>Building a navigation bar with evenly spaced items</li>
    <li>Creating a responsive card layout that wraps naturally</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>When should I use Grid instead of Flexbox?</strong> Flexbox excels at one-dimensional layouts (a single row or column); Grid is better suited for two-dimensional layouts with both rows and columns to control. See the <a href="/tools/grid-generator">Grid Generator</a> for a full comparison table.</p>
</ToolLayout>
